From: ayazkussan <ayazkussan@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 08:28:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] lubot: operator: kayit kurallari cagrisizlariyla gitti -
 kapinin isim listesi kirpildi

Three of operator.rs's four rules - compute bond, one model_hash across
active operators, the checkpoint transition window - were the purest case
the whole shrink loop has seen: a correct, tested, documented library for
records this tree never holds. No registration file, no operator set, no
window producer exists anywhere in the workspace; the accessors were
asserted only by their own fixtures (stripped by the gate's test rule), and
`old_active` was literally an alias calling `both_active`.

The mask this time was not a test or a doc line - it was a gate.
`operator-sync-rules` asserted the NAMES into existence: a shrink patch that
deleted them would fail the suite for the crime of having no caller while
keeping the rule, which is the reverse of what a ratchet is for. A gate
checking "does the string appear in a file" is the third masker this report
named in section 28, applied by us to ourselves; the fix is the same one used
for corpus claims - assert the behaviour that has a door, and let the rest
stay honestly absent. The gate now pins the effort-tier rule (wired: the CLI
checks tags at three doors, the chain client re-verifies the hashed tier)
and additionally refuses the deleted names if they return without callers -
the direction of the ratchet is preserved: growing back is only possible in
a patch that carries a caller, and the gate's docstring says so.

The README settled the discretion question for us: line 11 of the same file
assigns "operator registration, the compute bond ... the chain's AI
inference layer, ... in the node" - the crate's own scope section says these
rules are not Lubot's to run, while the tools table row advertised them as
if they were. Deleting the implementation makes both sentences true; the
table row now names only the effort ceiling, and its test count is the
measured 41, not the 47 it had claimed before either change (the row was two
tests stale when this patch started - recorded, because "recounted from the
applied tree" also means admitting what the recount finds).

Deleted: `compute_bond_ok`, `same_model_hash`, `OperatorRecord`,
`CheckpointWindow` with its impl (`both_active`, `old_active`,
`old_retired`), four tests, the gate's four name assertions (the effort
half and its self-test stand untouched), and the four baseline lines in the
same patch: 30 after this, from 34. Tree total: 328 tests by grep. The crate
header now records which three rules went, why, and what would bring them
back.

Not compiled: no cargo (measured); floor is gates rc=0 on the applied tree
(39/39 with the rewritten gate), read-through of every hunk, and a docs grep
proving no surviving sentence promises a deleted name.
